+**S3 object-key limit (#620).** The S3 gateway holds keys to S3's limit of 1,024 bytes of UTF-8. It measures the percent-decoded key alone: not its characters, not its encoded wire form, and not the flat `bucket/key` name it is stored under. Two operations enforce it: object `PUT`, where an object is created, and bulk `DeleteObjects`. An object `PUT` with a longer key is refused `400 KeyTooLongError` before any body byte is read. A `PUT` whose decoded bucket segment contains `/` is refused `400 InvalidBucketName`; otherwise `/b%2Fa/k` would store the same object as `/b/a/k` and move part of the key out of the measured segment. A bulk `DeleteObjects` that names a key over the limit is refused whole with `KeyTooLongError`, deleting none of its keys, which keeps every entry within the size the request-body cap is computed from. Single-object `GET`, `HEAD` and `DELETE` check neither rule, so an over-limit object stored before the limit existed, or written outside the S3 surface (`wyrd put --key`), stays readable and removable.
+
